WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.608
Firmware update capability is one of the
most important lifecycle features a device

2
00:00:04.688 --> 00:00:05.150
can have.

3
00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:07.834
It allows teams to fix defects, improve
reliability,

4
00:00:07.914 --> 00:00:10.691
and respond to changing requirements after
deployment.

5
00:00:10.771 --> 00:00:14.821
The update path is also highly sensitive
because it changes the software the device

6
00:00:14.901 --> 00:00:16.115
will trust in the future.

7
00:00:16.195 --> 00:00:20.072
Signed update concepts help the device
decide whether an update is authorized.

8
00:00:20.152 --> 00:00:23.739
At a safe engineering level, the design
should include integrity checks,

9
00:00:23.819 --> 00:00:26.885
metadata validation, version policy,
compatibility checks,

10
00:00:26.965 --> 00:00:30.205
and a clear decision about when an image
is considered ready to run.

11
00:00:30.285 --> 00:00:33.351
This course does not implement signing or
cryptographic code.

12
00:00:33.431 --> 00:00:37.076
Version and compatibility checks matter
because a valid image may still be

13
00:00:37.156 --> 00:00:38.544
inappropriate for a device.

14
00:00:38.624 --> 00:00:42.327
Hardware revision, storage layout, feature
set, regional configuration,

15
00:00:42.407 --> 00:00:46.398
migration state, and dependency versions
can all affect whether an update should

16
00:00:46.478 --> 00:00:46.941
proceed.

17
00:00:47.021 --> 00:00:49.683
Metadata should support those decisions
explicitly.

18
00:00:49.763 --> 00:00:51.845
Staging must handle power loss and resets.

19
00:00:51.925 --> 00:00:56.091
Many devices update in environments where
power can be interrupted or communication

20
00:00:56.171 --> 00:00:56.634
can fail.

21
00:00:56.714 --> 00:01:00.474
The firmware should define when data is
written, when metadata is committed,

22
00:01:00.554 --> 00:01:02.232
when the new image becomes active,

23
00:01:02.312 --> 00:01:04.857
and how the device recognizes an
incomplete update.

24
00:01:04.937 --> 00:01:06.731
Rollback policy should be explicit.

25
00:01:06.811 --> 00:01:10.051
Some products need the ability to return
to a previous valid image.

26
00:01:10.131 --> 00:01:12.850
Others may prohibit older versions for
policy reasons.

27
00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:15.764
Either way, the behavior should be
documented and tested.

28
00:01:15.844 --> 00:01:19.431
Ambiguous rollback behavior can leave the
device in a state that is hard to

29
00:01:19.511 --> 00:01:20.032
diagnose.

30
00:01:20.112 --> 00:01:23.236
Recovery modes need access controls and
operational planning.

31
00:01:23.316 --> 00:01:25.804
A recovery flow may be essential for field
service,

32
00:01:25.884 --> 00:01:29.876
but it should still respect production
policy, device identity, logging limits,

33
00:01:29.956 --> 00:01:31.171
and support procedures.

34
00:01:31.251 --> 00:01:34.433
Teams should define who can use recovery,
under what conditions,

35
00:01:34.513 --> 00:01:36.017
and what evidence is retained.

36
00:01:36.097 --> 00:01:39.742
Update systems deserve negative tests
before they are needed in the field.

37
00:01:39.822 --> 00:01:43.351
Test invalid metadata, incompatible
versions, interrupted transfer,

38
00:01:43.431 --> 00:01:46.613
storage exhaustion, reset during commit,
failed health checks,

39
00:01:46.693 --> 00:01:47.907
and recovery decisions.

40
00:01:47.987 --> 00:01:50.070
The desired outcome is predictable state,

41
00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:52.580
not merely a successful update in the
normal path.
